`BiConsumer<T,U>` accepts two arguments and returns nothing. Its primary use is `Map.forEach`:

```java
// Map.forEach takes BiConsumer<K, V>:
Map<String, BigDecimal> balances = Map.of("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("125000"),
    "CUR3001", new BigDecimal("850000"));

balances.forEach((accountId, balance) -> {
    log.info("Account {} has balance Rs.{}", accountId, balance);
});
// BiConsumer receives: (String key, BigDecimal value) → void

// Composition: andThen chains two BiConsumers
BiConsumer<String, BigDecimal> logIt = (id, bal) -> log.info("{}: {}", id, bal);
BiConsumer<String, BigDecimal> auditIt = (id, bal) -> auditService.record(id, bal);
BiConsumer<String, BigDecimal> both = logIt.andThen(auditIt);
balances.forEach(both);
```

`BiFunction<T,U,R>` takes two arguments and returns a result. Used in Map.merge, Map.compute, and Map.replaceAll:

```java
// Map.merge: BiFunction resolves key collision
Map<String, BigDecimal> totals = new HashMap<>();
trades.forEach(t -> {
    totals.merge(t.getSymbol(), t.getAmount(), BigDecimal::add);
    // merge(key, newValue, BiFunction<existing, newValue, result>)
    // If key exists: BiFunction decides (add amounts)
    // If key absent: put newValue directly
});

// Map.compute: BiFunction computes from key + existing value
balances.compute("SAV2001", (key, existingBalance) -> {
    return existingBalance == null ? BigDecimal.ZERO : existingBalance.add(interest);
});
// BiFunction receives: (String key, BigDecimal existingValueOrNull) → BigDecimal newValue

// Map.replaceAll: BiFunction transforms all values
balances.replaceAll((accountId, balance) -> balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("1.05")));
// Apply 5% interest to ALL balances
```

`BiPredicate<T,U>` takes two arguments and returns boolean. Used for two-input conditions:

```java
BiPredicate<Trade, BigDecimal> exceedsLimit = (trade, limit) ->
    trade.getAmount().compareTo(limit) > 0;

BiPredicate<String, String> sameAccount = String::equals;

// Composition:
BiPredicate<Trade, BigDecimal> exceedsAndSettled = exceedsLimit
    .and((trade, limit) -> trade.isSettled());

// Usage:
boolean result = exceedsLimit.test(trade, new BigDecimal("100000"));
```

